<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Southern Transitional Council</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2017">2017</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as STC</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Southern Transitional Council is a southern Yemeni separatist organisation formed in May 2017 under Aidarus al-Zoubaidi, the dismissed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, and backed by the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a>. It seeks an independent southern state. Its forces seized Aden from the recognised government in 2019, and it joined the Presidential Leadership Council in 2022 while keeping its own armed forces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/southern-transitional-council.jpg" alt="Southern Transitional Council" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Southern Transitional Council · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Yemen_(Non-standard_Variant).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Southern Transitional Council, also called the South Arabian Transitional Council, was formed as a faction of the Southern Movement. It called for the secession of a proposed federal State of South Arabia along the borders of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">South Yemen</a>, a name inspired by the British-created Federation of South Arabia. South Yemen had become independent in November 1967 and merged with the Yemen Arab Republic in May 1990. Southern separatists proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-yemen">Democratic Republic of Yemen</a> in May 1994, but the attempt was defeated that July.</p><p>On 27 April 2017 President Abdrabbuh Mansur Hadi dismissed Aydarus al-Zubaydi as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> because of his close ties to the United Arab Emirates. Large demonstrations followed, and on 4 May al-Zubaydi delivered the Aden Historic Declaration. With the support of the United Arab Emirates the council was formed on 11 May 2017 with him as chairman, and Hadi called it illegitimate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Fighting between the council's forces and those of the Hadi government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh-agreement">Riyadh Agreement</a> of 5 November 2019, which recognised the council and admitted it to the government in exchange for a military withdrawal from Aden. The council declared self-governance on 26 April 2020, which the government called a coup. In April 2022 it joined the Presidential Leadership Council, and al-Zubaydi became its vice president. At a congress in May 2023 the council adopted the Southern National Pact and raised its share of the Presidential Leadership Council to three of eight seats. In September 2025 it called for a two-state solution to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemeni-civil-war-2014-present">Yemeni civil war</a>.</p><p>On 2 December 2025 the council launched an offensive, codenamed Operation Promising Future, and by 8 December held most of the six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">governorates</a> of former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">South Yemen</a>. On 2 January 2026 it published a constitutional declaration for a State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a>. Saudi-backed forces of the Presidential Leadership Council counterattacked in early January 2026, retaking Hadhramaut and Al-Mahrah and then Abyan and Shabwah, and al-Zubaydi left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> on 7 January.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 9 January 2026 a delegation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> announced the dissolution of the council. Its official spokesman and other senior officials replied that the statement was null and void, made under duress, and that the council remained fully legitimate and operational. Since its loss of territory and removal from the Presidential Leadership Council, the group renamed itself the Transitional Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a> and went on staging protests. Several thousand of its supporters had already gathered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> in January 2026 despite a ban on demonstrations announced by Saudi-backed forces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Southern Transitional Council” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Today the Houthis rule Sanaa and most of the populous north, while the recognised government, led since April 2022 by a Presidential Leadership Council under Rashad al-Alimi, holds the south and east, where in January 2026 Saudi-backed forces took Aden from the separatist <b>Southern Transitional Council</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Republic of Yemen · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
